  #line-at(72.024pt, 238.7pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 257.66pt, "Second-harmonic generation microscopy is a powerful technique capable of probing local crystal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 276.62pt, "symmetries and electric fields at interfaces. However, it often suffers from weak signal strength and is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 295.73pt, "difficult to understand in multilayer systems where many materials can give competing signal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 314.69pt, "contributions. In this work we present direct observation of delocalized, surface plasmon polariton-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 333.65pt, "mediated second-harmonic generation on gold monocrystalline surfaces and structures. We generate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.61pt, "second-harmonic light up to 35 µm from the excitation spot and, excitingly, we obtain signal from ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 371.57pt, "atomically flat surfaces without a fundamental excitation beam present in the same region. We reveal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 390.53pt, "that this process arises from the interaction of two counter-propagating surface plasmon polaritons, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 409.49pt, "which we believe to be the first observation of this process at the microscale. This signal has the same ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 428.45pt, "polarisation dependence as localised second-harmonic generation and is emitted in a collimated beam ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 447.41pt, "travelling perpendicular to the sample surface. In part due to local electric field enhancements, we were ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 466.51pt, "able to observe these signals on a CMOS camera with 1 s exposure and no gain using an industrial-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 485.47pt, "grade pulsed laser. Our results enable wide area multilayer samples to be probed using a single ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 504.43pt, "excitation beam, with applications including in energy, catalysis and single particle surface sensing. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 542.35pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 561.43pt, "Second-harmonic generation (SHG), two photons of angular frequency ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(393.19pt, 561.43pt, "𝜔")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(401.47pt, 561.43pt, " generating a single photon ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 580.75pt, "with frequency 2")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(146.9pt, 580.75pt, "𝜔")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(155.18pt, 580.75pt, ", is a nonlinear optical process that was first observed from a bulk nonlinear crystal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 600.07pt, "in 1961 [1] and a year later  from a surface of a centrosymmetric material [2]. SHG has been employed ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 619.03pt, "in laser devices for frequency conversion, high-resolution microscopy for biological and medical ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 637.99pt, "sciences, and as a spectroscopic probe in a wide variety of fields, including for measuring the orientation ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 656.98pt, "of crystal grains, sensing the static electric field at the surface of materials and measuring asymmetry ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 675.94pt, "and crystal defects [3–5]. One of the reasons SHG is an extremely powerful spectroscopic tool is that, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 694.9pt, "for centrosymmetric materials, it originates from surfaces or defects with near-vanishing contributions ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 713.86pt, "from the bulk of the sample [6]. This is because second-order nonlinear processes are allowed only in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 732.82pt, "non-centrosymmetric materials, while at the interface of any two materials or at the location of a defect ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 751.776pt, "this inversion symmetry is naturally broken [7]. As a result, surface SHG microscopy is especially ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-02.png", width: 6.5pt), 342.5pt, 514.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 803.976pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.32pt, "useful in unravelling surface sensitive effects, including in photovoltaics and probing electrical double ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 101.3pt, "layers effects in catalysis, where the role of surfaces is vital for nearly all aspects of device ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 120.26pt, "function [8,9]. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 158.18pt, "As the amplitude of SHG is proportional to the square of the local (optical) electric field, it can be ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 177.26pt, "augmented using dielectric or plasmonic resonances that give rise to substantial local field ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 196.22pt, "enhancement [10]. In particular, orders of magnitude SHG enhancements have been reported in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 215.18pt, "individual nanostructures and in metasurfaces [11–20]. This in turn enables far easier observation of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 234.14pt, "SHG from samples with weak nonlinear response. For instance, in nanoparticles, the angular ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 253.1pt, "dependence of SHG has been exploited to probe their symmetry, enabling a diagnostic tool for verifying ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 272.06pt, "whether a nanoparticle exhibits the expected shape and size [21]. However, while localized surface ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 291.05pt, "plasmon resonance (LSPR) enhanced SHG has now been extensively studied, enhancing SHG via ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.01pt, "delocalized, propagating surface plasmons has remained largely unexplored [22].   ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 348.05pt, "Two key issues prevent SHG, and especially SHG microscopy, being more widely applied as an ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 367.01pt, "interfacial probe. Firstly, SHG is a weak signal that, except with specialised set-ups optimized for a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 385.97pt, "particular task (e.g. examining surface reconstruction) [8], can only be detected from a region close to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 404.93pt, "the (sub-micron) excitation spot on a sample surface at any time, limiting its application for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 423.89pt, "understanding wide sample regions. Secondly, when light is incident on a multilayer structure (for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 442.85pt, "example a solar cell or battery) every interface and non-centrosymmetric (and possible other weak bulk ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 461.81pt, "effects [23]) can contribute to the SHG, making it challenging to disentangle competing contributions.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 499.75pt, "In this work we present delocalized SHG achieved through propagating surface plasmon polaritons ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 519.55pt, "(SPPs). Plasmon-enhanced SHG is observed as far as 35 ")
  #line-at(348.67pt, 519.55pt, "m away from the excitation spot and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 538.99pt, "generated across a monocrystalline gold surface. This effect is facilitated by the interaction of counter-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 557.95pt, "propagating SPPs with no or minimal concurrent emission of the light at the fundamental frequency. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 576.91pt, "This yields SHG across a large monocrystalline area quasi-simultaneously (< ps) using solely point ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 595.87pt, "source illumination. In contrast to prior works on plasmonic nanoantennas or waveguides [24–28], ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 614.83pt, "which exhibited size-dependent response in terms of amplitude and spatial emission, we demonstrate a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 633.91pt, "strong signal from unpatterned gold surfaces with nearly-uniform spatial intensity, regardless of the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 652.87pt, "spatial separation between the gratings. This renders our method more suitable for generic dielectric-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 671.86pt, "metal interfaces. Uniquely, we also demonstrate highly directional emission perpendicular to the gold ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 690.82pt, "surface, in stark contrast with SHG light scattered from plasmonic nanostructures over a wide range of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 709.78pt, "angles [29]. The high signal strength allowed us to observe SHG light using a low-cost CMOS camera ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 728.74pt, "with 1 s exposure times and an industrial-grade pulsed laser with ~ 1 nJ per pulse (40 MHz repetition ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 747.696pt, "rate, <200 fs pulse width).  We find that on a monocrystalline gold surface the SPP-induced SHG signal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 766.656pt, "follows the same sensitivity to the local crystal structure as locally excited SHG. Our results prove that ")
]

