#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 803.976pt, "5 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.32pt, "power is reflected at 1030 nm. This creates two counter-propagating SPPs waves and allows us to study ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 101.3pt, "effects arising due to their interference. An image of the structure fabricated on a monocrystalline gold ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 120.26pt, "surface is shown in Figure 2b. Figure 2c shows the intense reflection of the fundamental light from the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 139.22pt, "excitation spot and only weak scattering from the left edge of the reflection grating. The SHG signal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 158.18pt, "from this structure is shown in Figure 2d on a logarithmic scale. Excitingly, in addition to the SHG ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 177.26pt, "signal from the two gratings, similar to the described-above measurements, we detect SHG emission ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 196.22pt, "from the unpatterned surface between them. At the same time, we do not observe any significant ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 215.18pt, "fundamental beam emission from the region between the two gratings, apart from some minor speckles ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 234.14pt, "due to the scattering of the excitation laser beam. This is further demonstrated in the line profile in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 253.1pt, "Figure 2e, where the fundamental and SHG signals are overlaid, both normalised to their strength at the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 272.06pt, "outcoupling grating. While the fundamental signal drops to less than 5 % of this maximum just a few ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 291.05pt, "microns away from the grating, the SHG signal remains larger than 30 % of the maximum across the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.01pt, "entire unpatterned region. This implies that the interference of the two counter-propagating SPPs, which ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 328.97pt, "are confined at the interface at the fundamental frequency, results in SHG that is observable in the far-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 348.05pt, "field (see discussion of the physical mechanism below). We observe similar results for reflection ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 367.01pt, "gratings spaced from 10 µm to 35 µm from the in-coupling grating, as is presented in Supplementary ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 385.97pt, "Information Note 4. Thus, we demonstrate not only spatial decoupling of the SHG and fundamental ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 404.93pt, "signals on the surface, but also conversion of the intrinsically evanescent wave to a free-space ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 423.89pt, "propagating wave, which is not attainable with a single SPP, as demonstrated above. As we show next, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 442.85pt, "this opens unprecedented opportunities for the study of buried interfaces.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 480.79pt, "While SHG is a powerful probe for studying single interfaces, in multilayer structures it can be ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 499.75pt, "challenging to assess which interfaces contribute to the signal, as noted above. We now demonstrate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 518.83pt, "how delocalized, SPP-induced SHG can access a specific buried interface without use of non-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 537.79pt, "centrosymmetric materials [35]. To this end, following the schematic in Figure 2f, we again fabricated ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 556.75pt, "in-coupling and reflective gratings on the monocrystalline gold surface, but this time we also sputtered ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 575.71pt, "a ~2 µm-thick SiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(154.7pt, 576.67pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.18pt, 575.71pt, " layer on top of gold, creating a buried gold/glass interface. A white-light image of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 594.67pt, "the resulting structure is presented in Figure 2g.  As observed in the prior experiment, the signal at the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 613.63pt, "fundamental wavelength is still strongest at the in-coupling grating (Figure 2h). Remarkably, the SHG ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 632.59pt, "signal is clearly visible over the entire region of interest even on a linear scale and the strongest SHG ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 651.55pt, "signal is now observed at the reflection grating (Figure 2i), located 20 μm away. This is further ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 670.54pt, "emphasised in the line profiles in Figure 2j. We also note that all SHG signals were significantly ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 689.62pt, "enhanced relative to those in air (for the region between the gratings, approximately by a factor of 5 in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 708.58pt, "intensity), to the point that we could record the image in Figure 2i using an inexpensive colour CMOS ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 727.54pt, "microscopy camera with 1 s exposure time. SHG has been previously shown to be enhanced by ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 746.496pt, "dielectric layers on metals due to more favourable Fresnel reflection/transmission coefficients at ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 765.456pt, "interfaces [36]. Here we suggest further improvements are due to increased SPP vertical confinement, ")
]

