  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 220.34pt, "Supporting Information Note 1 – reflection measurements on gratings ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 241.94pt, "We carried out white light reflection measurements on the in-coupling gratings, referencing our signal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 260.9pt, "to that of bare gold, for light incident with polarisation both parallel and perpendicular to the grating ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 280.01pt, "axis (following methods in our previous work) [1]. Results are presented in Figure S1. As expected, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 298.97pt, "when the electric field polarisation is perpendicular to the grating there is significantly less reflection, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 317.93pt, "due to the excitation or surface plasmon polaritons. More specifically, our measurements show a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 336.89pt, "broadband reduction in reflection between ~700 nm and ~ 1100 nm, compared to the parallel ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 355.85pt, "polarisation case. We attribute this to our gratings being square, meaning they include many optical ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 374.81pt, "frequencies. We designed our gratings to excite surface plasmon polaritons at 1030 nm. At this ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 393.77pt, "wavelength, our measurements suggest up to 20 % of the incident beam couples to surface plasmon ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 412.73pt, "polaritons.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(330.07pt, 466.75pt, "Figure S1. Reflection of light incident ")
  #line-at(330.07pt, 485.71pt, "perpendicular to the sample plane, relative ")
  #line-at(330.07pt, 504.67pt, "to reflection from a bare gold surface, for ")
  #line-at(330.07pt, 523.63pt, "the incident electric field being polarised ")
  #line-at(330.07pt, 542.59pt, "parallel (black) and perpendicular (red) to ")
  #line-at(330.07pt, 561.55pt, "the grating. ")
]

// arXiv source page 20
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-20.png", width: 411pt), 89pt, 337pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 803.976pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.32pt, "Supporting Information Note 2 – confirmation of second-harmonic generation ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 125.66pt, "We confirmed we are observing second-harmonic generation through spectral and power-dependant ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 144.62pt, "measurements, as presented in Figure S2.")
  #line-at(261.29pt, 144.62pt, "For all samples measured we recorded the spectrum of the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 163.58pt, "detected signal after a 750 nm dichroic shortpass mirror and two 850 nm shortpass filters (Methods). In ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 182.54pt, "Figure S2a, we present these results both for regions surrounding the grating and for the signal between ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 201.5pt, "gratings (when employing a reflection grating). It can be seen both signals have a strong peak at the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 220.46pt, "SHG wavelength (515 nm) and a weak and broad two-photon photoluminescence signal at longer ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 239.54pt, "wavelengths, consistent with previous reports [2]. In spatially resolved measurements we used a 515 nm ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 258.5pt, "bandpass filter to remove the contribution from two photon photoluminescence (Methods). We also ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 277.49pt, "recorded the power scaling of the SHG signal with laser power, and present our results in Figure S2b ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 296.45pt, "on a log-log scale. We fit with a straight line and obtain a coefficient of 1.95 i.e. implying counts are ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 315.41pt, "quadratic with laser power, confirming SHG.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 523.75pt, "Figure S2. a) Spectrally resolved SHG signal and b) log-log scaling of signal at 515 nm with laser ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 542.71pt, "power, for both a region surrounding an in-coupling grating, and the region between an in-coupling ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 561.67pt, "grating and a reflection grating. Data presented here is for a gold/SiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(383.23pt, 562.63pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(386.71pt, 561.67pt, " interface. ")
]

// arXiv source page 21
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-21.png", width: 451pt), 72pt, 348pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 803.976pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.32pt, "Supporting Information Note 3 – finite element method modelling of reflector grating ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.94pt, "To confirm the role of our reflection gratings, we used finite element method modelling of a gold-air ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 123.02pt, "interface. We analytically defined the SPP travelling across the surface, and we present the electric field ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 141.98pt, "amplitude perpendicular to the surface in Figure S3a. We then recorded how this field changes as ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 160.94pt, "‘reflection gratings’ are introduced into the simulation. By comparing Figure S3a (no grating) and b ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 179.9pt, "(with grating), the electric field amplitude prior to the gratings (noting SPPs are incident from the left ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 198.86pt, "side of the simulation) is seen to increase in the latter case. Conversely, in Figures S3c and d we present ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 217.82pt, "the time-averaged power flow of the electric field in the original direction of SPP propagation without ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 236.78pt, "and with the grating present. When the grating is introduced we observe a reduction in the power flow ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 255.74pt, "(a reduction of 30-40 %). This is explained by a significant portion of the SPP being reflected by the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 274.7pt, "grating, reducing the time averaged power flow while increasing the field amplitude (i.e. a partial ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 293.81pt, "standing wave). By placing a port in our simulations at the left hand edge of the simulation and recording ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 312.77pt, "the time averaged power flow through it both with and without the grating, we were able to calculate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.73pt, "that approximately 33 % of the power is reflected into a backwards propagating SPP by the grating. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 440.81pt, "Figure S3. COMSOL simulations of a surface plasmon polariton travelling along a gold/air interface ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 459.77pt, "from left to right. Specifically, the surface perpendicular component of the electric field amplitude (a, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 478.87pt, "b) and the surface parallel time averaged power flow (c, d) for a gold/air interface are shown for the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 497.83pt, "case without (left) and with (right) a reflection grating in the simulation. Outer boundaries in all ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 516.79pt, "simulations are perfectly matched layers. ")
]

